            <ArticleTitle>Internal jugular vein thrombosis after cervical traction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>333-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A clinically and roentgenographically typical case of internal jugular vein thrombosis is presented in a patient having undergone repeated sessions of Glisson traction for neck and backache. In this mode of therapy, axial traction is applied to the neck by attaching a weight, via a pulley, to a construction of straps encompassing the head. This particular trauma to the neck has not previously been described to be associated with spontaneous internal jugular vein thrombosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
